\fs64 \cf2 3. Create and use variables\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Let's extend this basic program by prompting the user for their name and including it in the greeting. The built-in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/io/io/input/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 input()}} function accepts an optional {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/string/string/String/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 String}} argument to use as a prompt and returns a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  consisting of the characters the user entered (with the newline character at the end stripped off).\
Let's declare a variable, assign the return value from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 input()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to it, and build a customized greeting.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 life.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf16  raises\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  name\cf5 :\cf16  String \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 input\cf5 (\cf23 "Who are you? "\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  greeting\cf5 :\cf16  String \cf5 =\cf16  \cf23 "Hi, "\cf16  \cf5 +\cf16  name \cf5 +\cf16  \cf23 "!"\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf16 greeting\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Go ahead and run it:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 mojo life.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 Who are you? Edna\cb1 \
\cb15 Hi, Edna!\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Notice that this code uses a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type annotation that indicates the type of value the variable can contain. The Mojo compiler performs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_system#Static_type_checking"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 static type checking}}, which means you'll encounter a compile-time error if your code tries to assign a value of one type to a variable of a different type.\
Mojo also supports implicitly declared variables, where you simply assign a value to a new variable without using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword or indicating its type. We can replace the code we just entered with the following, and it works exactly the same.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 life.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf16  raises\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15     name \cf5 =\cf16  \cf14 input\cf5 (\cf23 "Who are you? "\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     greeting \cf5 =\cf16  \cf23 "Hi, "\cf16  \cf5 +\cf16  name \cf5 +\cf16  \cf23 "!"\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf16 greeting\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 However, implicitly declared variables still have a fixed type, which Mojo automatically infers from the initial value assignment. In this example, both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 greeting
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are inferred as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type variables. If you then try to assign an integer value like 42 to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable, you'll get a compile-time error because of the type mismatch. You can learn more about Mojo variables in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/variables/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Variables}} section of the Mojo manual.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 4. Use Mojo 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  types to represent the game state\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As originally envisioned by John Conway, the game's "world" is an infinite, two-dimensional grid of square cells, but for our implementation, we'll constrain the grid to a finite size. A drawback of making the edges of the grid a hard boundary is that there are fewer neighboring cells around the edges compared to the interior, which tends to cause die-offs. Therefore, we'll model the world as a toroid (a donut shape), where the top row is considered adjacent to the bottom row, and the left column is considered adjacent to the right column. This will come into play later when we implement the algorithm for calculating each subsequent generation.\
To keep track of the height and width of our grid, we'll use {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/Int/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Int}}, which represents a signed integer of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_(computer_architecture)"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 word size}} of the CPU, typically 32 or 64 bits.\
To represent the state of an individual cell, we'll use an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value of 1 (populated) or 0 (unpopulated). Later, when we need to determine the number of populated neighbors surrounding a cell, we can simply add the values of the neighboring cells.\
To represent the state of the entire grid, we need a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/types/#collection-types"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 collection type}}. The most appropriate for this use case is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/list/List/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 List}}, which is a dynamically-sized sequence of values.\
All values in a Mojo 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  must be the same type so the Mojo compiler can ensure type safety. (For example, when we retrieve a value from a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[Int]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the compiler knows the value is an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and can verify that we use it correctly.) Mojo collections are implemented as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generic_programming"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 generic types}}, so we can indicate the type of values the specific collection will hold by specifying a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/#parameterized-structs"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 type parameter}} in square brackets like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb15 # The List in row can contain only Int values\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15 row \cf5 =\cf16  List\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ]()\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb15 # The List in names can contain only String values\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15 names \cf5 =\cf16  List\cf5 [\cf16 String\cf5 ]()\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 We can also create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with an initial set of values and let the compiler infer the type. Use the list literal syntax and simply enclose the values in square brackets (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 []
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb15 # Create a List[Int] with the list literal syntax, inferring the type\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15 nums2 \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 [\cf17 12\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf17 7\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 64\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb15 # which is equivalent to\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15 nums2\cf5 :\cf16  List\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 [\cf17 12\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf17 7\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 64\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The Mojo 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type includes the ability to append to the list, pop values from the list, and access list items using subscript notation. Here's a taste of those operations:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 nums \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 [\cf17 12\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf17 7\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 64\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15 nums\cf5 .\cf16 append\cf5 (-\cf17 937\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf23 "Number of elements in the list:"\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf14 len\cf5 (\cf16 nums\cf5 ))\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf23 "Popping last element off the list:"\cf5 ,\cf16  nums\cf5 .\cf16 pop\cf5 ())\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf23 "First element of the list:"\cf5 ,\cf16  nums\cf5 [\cf17 0\cf5 ])\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf23 "Second element of the list:"\cf5 ,\cf16  nums\cf5 [\cf17 1\cf5 ])\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf23 "Last element of the list:"\cf5 ,\cf16  nums\cf5 [-\cf17 1\cf5 ])\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 Number of elements in the list: 4\cb1 \
\cb15 Popping last element off the list: -937\cb1 \
\cb15 First element of the list: 12\cb1 \
\cb15 Second element of the list: -7\cb1 \
\cb15 Last element of the list: 64\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 We can also nest 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 grid \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 [\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 [\cf17 11\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 22\cf5 ],\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 [\cf17 33\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 44\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf23 "Row 0, Column 0:"\cf5 ,\cf16  grid\cf5 [\cf17 0\cf5 ][\cf17 0\cf5 ])\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf23 "Row 0, Column 1:"\cf5 ,\cf16  grid\cf5 [\cf17 0\cf5 ][\cf17 1\cf5 ])\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf23 "Row 1, Column 0:"\cf5 ,\cf16  grid\cf5 [\cf17 1\cf5 ][\cf17 0\cf5 ])\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf23 "Row 1, Column 1:"\cf5 ,\cf16  grid\cf5 [\cf17 1\cf5 ][\cf17 1\cf5 ])\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
