\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 Row 0, Column 0: 11\cb1 \
\cb15 Row 0, Column 1: 22\cb1 \
\cb15 Row 1, Column 0: 33\cb1 \
\cb15 Row 1, Column 1: 44\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This looks like a good way to represent the state of the grid for our program. Let's update the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function with the following code that defines an 8\'d78 grid containing the initial state of a "{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glider_(Conway%27s_Game_of_Life)"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 glider}}" pattern.\
Notice that we've removed 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . In our earlier steps, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  called 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 input()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which can raise an error, so it needed the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword. Mojo functions are non-raising by default\'97you only need 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when a function can propagate an error to its caller. Since 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  no longer calls any raising functions, we can omit it. For more about error handling, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/errors/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Errors, error handling, and context managers}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

