\fs48 \cf2 Copying values in Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Before we move on, let's take a moment to discuss how Mojo handles copying values. There's an important difference in Mojo between copying simple types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and more complex types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
An explicitly copyable type can be copied by calling its copy initializer or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is explicitly copyable, so if 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 first
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you can copy it like this:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 first \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 [\cf17 1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 3\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf16 \cb15 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
second \cf5 =\cf16  first\cf5 .\cf16 copy\cf5 ()\cf16   \cf6 # explicit copy\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf16 \cb15 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf18 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf19 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cb15 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 This leaves 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 first
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  unchanged and assigns 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 second
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  its own, uniquely owned copy of the list.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
An implicitly copyable type can be copied without an explicit call to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or a copy initializer. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are implicitly copyable types, so if 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 one_value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you can copy it like this:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 one_value \cf5 =\cf16  \cf17 15\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf16 \cb15 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
another_value \cf5 =\cf16  one_value  \cf6 # implicit copy\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf16 \cb15 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf18 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf19 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cb15 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 Here, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 one_value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is unchanged, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 another_value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  gets a copy of the value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Implicit copying is useful for simple types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , where copying is inexpensive and has no side effects. In contrast, a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  might occupy megabytes of memory, and unintentionally copying it could be a significant performance hit. Therefore, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type supports only explicit copying to prevent accidental copying. Understanding this distinction will be important when we define and use our own custom types later in this tutorial.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 You can determine whether a type is explicitly or implicitly copyable by checking its API documentation to see what traits it conforms to. A Mojo {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/"}}{\fldrslt \ul trait}} is a set of requirements that a type must implement, usually in the form of one or more method signatures.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 List
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  type and other Mojo collection types like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/dict/Dict/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul Dict}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/set/Set/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul Set}} conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Copyable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul Copyable}} trait, which indicates that they are explicitly copyable.\cb1 \
\ls9\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 String
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Int
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  and other numeric types conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/ImplicitlyCopyable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 \ul ImplicitlyCopyable}} trait, which indicates that they are implicitly copyable. Additionally, the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 ImplicitlyCopyable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  trait refines the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Copyable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Movable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  traits, so you can also use types that conform to the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 ImplicitlyCopyable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  trait in the same way you can use types that conform to the other traits.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 5. Create and use a function to print the grid\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Now let's create a function using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword to generate a string representation of the game grid so we can print it to the terminal. To learn more about defining functions in Mojo, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Functions}}.\
Let's add the following definition of a function named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 grid_str()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to our program. The Mojo compiler doesn't care whether we add our function before or after 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but the convention is to put 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  at the end.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 life.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf14 grid_str\cf5 (\cf16 rows\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  cols\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  grid\cf5 :\cf16  List\cf5 [\cf16 List\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ]])\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  String\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15     \cf6 # Create an empty String\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf14 str\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  String\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf6 # Iterate through rows 0 through rows-1\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 for\cf16  row \cf5 in\cf16  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf16 rows\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # Iterate through columns 0 through cols-1\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 for\cf16  col \cf5 in\cf16  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf16 cols\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf5 if\cf16  grid\cf5 [\cf16 row\cf5 ][\cf16 col\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf17 1\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 \cf14 str\cf16  \cf5 +=\cf16  \cf23 "*"\cf16   \cf6 # If cell is populated, append an asterisk\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf5 else:\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 \cf14 str\cf16  \cf5 +=\cf16  \cf23 " "\cf16   \cf6 # If cell is not populated, append a space\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 if\cf16  row \cf5 !=\cf16  rows\cf5 -\cf17 1\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf14 str\cf16  \cf5 +=\cf16  \cf23 "\\n"\cf16      \cf6 # Add a newline between rows, but not at the end\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 return\cf16  \cf14 str\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When we pass a value to a Mojo function, the default behavior is that an argument is treated as a read-only reference to the value. This is particularly useful for values like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s, where copying them could be expensive. As we'll see later, we can specify different behavior by including an explicit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 argument convention}}.\
Each argument name is followed by a type annotation indicating the type of value you can pass to the argument. Just like when assigning a value to a variable, you'll encounter a compile-time error if your code tries to pass a value of one type to an argument of a different type. Finally, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -> String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  following the argument list indicates that this function has a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type return value.\
In the body of the function, we generate a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  by appending an asterisk for each populated cell and a space for each unpopulated cell, separating each row of the grid with a newline character. We use nested 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loops to iterate through each row and column of the grid, using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/range/range/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 range()}} to generate a sequence of integers from 0 up to (but not including) the given end value. Then we append the correct characters to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  representation. See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/control-flow/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Control flow}} for more information about 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and other control flow structures in Mojo.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 As described in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/control-flow/#the-for-statement"}}{\fldrslt \ul The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb20 for
\fs28 \cb13  statement}} section of the Mojo manual, it's possible to iterate over the elements of a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 List
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  directly instead of iterating over the values of a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 range()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  and then accessing the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 List
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  elements by their numeric index.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf16 \cb20 nums \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 [\cf17 12\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 -\cf17 7\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 64\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb20 for\cf16  value \cf5 in\cf16  nums\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb20     \cf5 print(\cf23 "Value:"\cf5 ,\cf16  value\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

