\fs64 \cf2 6. Create a module and define a custom type\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 We're currently passing three arguments to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 grid_str()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to describe the size and state of the grid to print. A better approach would be to define our own custom type that encapsulates all information about the grid. Then any function that needs to manipulate a grid can accept a single argument. We can do this by defining a Mojo struct, which is a custom data structure.\
A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/structs/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Mojo struct}} is a custom type consisting of:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Fields, which are variables containing the data associated with the structure\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Methods, which are functions that we can optionally define to manipulate instances of the struct that we create\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Optionally, a set of traits that the struct conforms to\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 Mojo structs are similar to classes. However, Mojo structs do not support inheritance. Mojo doesn't support classes at this time.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 We could define the struct in our existing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 life.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  source file, but let's create a separate module for the struct. A module is simply a Mojo source file containing struct and function definitions that can be imported into other Mojo source files. To learn more about creating and importing modules, see the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/packages/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Modules and packages}} section of the Mojo manual.\
Create a new source file named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gridv1.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in the project directory containing the following definition of a struct named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with three fields:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 gridv1.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf14 Grid\cf5 (\cf16 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  rows\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  cols\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf16  data\cf5 :\cf16  List\cf5 [\cf16 List\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ]]\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo requires you to declare all fields in the struct definition. You can't add fields dynamically at run-time. You must declare the type for each field, but you cannot assign a value as part of the field declaration.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#constructor"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 constructor}} is responsible for initializing the value of all fields, as well as allocating additional resources and performing any other configuration required by a new instance of the struct. You implement a constructor by defining a method named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in the struct definition. Here's an example of how we could implement the constructor for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf14 __init__\cf5 (out\cf16  self\cf5 ,\cf16  rows\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  cols\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 var\cf16  data\cf5 :\cf16  List\cf5 [\cf16 List\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ]]):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 rows \cf5 =\cf16  rows\cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 cols \cf5 =\cf16  cols\cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 data \cf5 =\cf16  data\cf5 ^\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The first argument of a constructor is the newly created instance of the struct, which by convention is named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The Mojo compiler automatically passes the instance to the constructor when you create a new instance of the struct. Note that in a constructor, you must include the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 argument convention}} for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument. The values of the remaining arguments are assigned to the corresponding fields of the new instance. (Don't worry about the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  character for now. We'll discuss both of them in more detail later.)\
To reduce the amount of boilerplate code you need to write, Mojo provides a decorator called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/decorators/fieldwise-init/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 @fieldwise_init}} that automatically generates a constructor for you that performs "field-wise" initialization. The constructor's arguments have the same names and types as the struct's fields and appear in the same order. This means that given our original definition of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , we can create an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 my_grid \cf5 =\cf16  Grid\cf5 (\cf17 2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 2\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf5 [[\cf17 0\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 1\cf5 ],\cf16  \cf5 [\cf17 1\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 1\cf5 ]])\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 We can then access the field values with "dot" syntax like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 print(\cf23 "Rows:"\cf5 ,\cf16  my_grid\cf5 .\cf16 rows\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 Rows: 2\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 However, we also need to be able to copy and move instances of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \'97for example, when we pass an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to a function or method. Mojo structs support several different {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 lifecycle methods}} that define the behavior when an instance of the struct is created, moved, copied, and destroyed.\
Structs that conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Movable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Movable}} {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 trait}} denote a type whose value can be moved, and structs that conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Copyable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Copyable}} trait denote a type whose value can be explicitly copied and/or moved. You can then implement custom move and copy constructors that perform the necessary operations to move or copy the instance.\
As a convenience for structs that are basic aggregations of other types and don't require custom resource management or lifecycle behaviors, you can simply indicate that the struct conforms to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  traits without implementing the corresponding lifecycle methods. In that case, the Mojo compiler automatically generates the missing methods for you. For our simple 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct, indicating that it conforms to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait is enough to have the Mojo compiler automatically generate the missing methods for us. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait also provides a default implementation of the copy initializer and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so you don't need to implement it yourself.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 If you define a simple struct where all fields are types that conform to the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 ImplicitlyCopyable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  trait\'97such as 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 String
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  and numeric types\'97you could indicate that your struct conforms to the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 ImplicitlyCopyable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  trait instead of the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Copyable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  trait. However, our 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Grid
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  struct uses a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 List[List[Int]]
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  field, which is not implicitly copyable.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb13 Also see the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Life of a value}} section of the Mojo Manual for more information about the different lifecycle methods and how to implement them.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 7. Import a module and use our custom 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  type\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

