\cf2 All these changes are straightforward except for the line where we create the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance. Our new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  needs to take ownership of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[List[Int]]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  representing the grid state. (Technically, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  will own the value.) However, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 glider
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable currently owns the list.\
One alternative\'97if we plan to use the value of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 glider
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable again later in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \'97would be to create a copy of the 
