\ls11\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The function definition is indented to indicate that it's a method defined by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct. This also changes how we invoke the function. Instead of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 grid_str(my_grid)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , we now write 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 my_grid.grid_str()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
We've changed the argument name to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . When you invoke an instance method, Mojo automatically passes the instance as the first argument, followed by any explicit arguments you provide. Although we could use any name we like for this argument, the convention is to call it 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
We've deleted the argument's type annotation. The compiler knows the first argument of the method is an instance of the struct, so it doesn't require an explicit type annotation.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
We don't need to add an explicit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 argument convention}} to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
