\fs64 \cf2 9. Implement support for the 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  trait\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can convert most Mojo types to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String(my_val)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to produce a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  representation of that instance. However, you'll get an error if you try to do that with our current implementation of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Let's fix that.\
Because the Mojo compiler performs static type checking, a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor can accept a value only if its type implements some required behavior\'97in this case, it only accepts types that can generate a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  representation.\
To enforce this, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructors require a type to conform to the [
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ] trait. (This type of function is sometimes referred to as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/#parameters-and-generics"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 generic function}}.) Each trait requires a conforming type to implement a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 write_to()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that writes its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  representation. (To learn more, read {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/#the-stringable-representable-and-writable-traits"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 The 
\fs28 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cb1  trait}}.)\
Notice that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 write_to()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  takes a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut writer: Some[Writer]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait represents anything that can be written to\'97
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  buffers, files, network streams, and so on. By writing to a generic 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instead of building and returning a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , your type works with any output destination.\
Our 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 grid_str()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method already write our 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  representation, so it looks like we'll have to rename it to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 write_to()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument to the function, and remove the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return value. We also need to indicate which trait 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conforms to. In our case, it must be 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
In 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gridv1.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , we need to update the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  declaration to indicate that the type conforms to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and update the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 grid_str()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 write_to()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

