\fs64 \cf2 10. Implement methods to support indexing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Looking at the implementation of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __str__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you'll notice that we use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self.data[row][col]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to retrieve the value of a cell in the grid. If 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 my_grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , we would use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 my_grid.data[row][col]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to refer to a cell in the grid. This breaks a fundamental principle of encapsulation because we need to know that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  stores the game state in a field called 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and that field is a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[List[Int]]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . If we later decide to change the internal implementation of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , there could be a lot of code that would need to be changed.\
A cleaner approach is to provide "getter" and "setter" methods to access cell values. We could simply define methods like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 get_cell()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 set_cell()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but this is a good opportunity to show how we can define the behavior of built-in operators for custom Mojo types. Specifically, we'll implement support for indexing so we can refer to a cell with syntax like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 my_grid[row, col]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . This will be useful when we implement support for evolving the state of the grid.\
As described in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/operators/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Operators, expressions, and dunder methods}}, Mojo allows us to define the behavior of many built-in operators for a custom type by implementing special dunder (double underscore) methods. In the case of indexing, the two methods are 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Let's add the following methods to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gridv1.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 gridv1.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf14 Grid\cf5 (\cf16 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf16  Stringable\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15     \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf14 __getitem__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 ,\cf16  row\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  col\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  Int\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 data\cf5 [\cf16 row\cf5 ][\cf16 col\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf14 __setitem__\cf5 (mut\cf16  self\cf5 ,\cf16  row\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  col\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  value\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  \cf17 None\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 data\cf5 [\cf16 row\cf5 ][\cf16 col\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  value\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The implementation of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is straightforward. For the given values of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 row
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 col
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , we just need to retrieve and return the corresponding value from the nested 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[List[Int]]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  stored in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field of the instance.\
The body of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is similarly straightforward. We just take the given 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and store it in the corresponding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 row
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 col
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . One new thing in the declaration is that we set the return type to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 None
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to indicate that the method doesn't have a return value. More notable is that we've added the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 argument convention}} to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument to explicitly tell the Mojo compiler that we want to mutate the state of the current instance. If we were to omit 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , we would get an error because the compiler would default to read-only access for the argument.\
Now that we've implemented these methods, we can update 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __str__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to use indexing syntax to access the cell value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 gridv1.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf14 Grid\cf5 (\cf16 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf16  Stringable\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15     \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 fn\cf16  \cf14 __str__\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  String\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf6 # Iterate through columns 0 through cols-1\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf5 for\cf16  col \cf5 in\cf16  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 .\cf16 cols\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 \cf5 if\cf16  self\cf5 [\cf16 row\cf5 ,\cf16  col\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf17 1\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

