\fs64 \cf2 11. Define a static method to generate random grids\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 So far, we've used the glider to build the basic functionality of our 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type. However, what's much more interesting is to start with a grid in a random state and see how it evolves over time.\
Let's add a static method named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 random()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct to generate and return an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with a random state. A static method doesn't operate on specific instances of the type, so it can be invoked as a utility function. We indicate that a method is static by using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @staticmethod
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 gridv1.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 import\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 random\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf14 Grid\cf5 (\cf16 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf16  Stringable\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15     \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 @staticmethod\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf14 random\cf5 (\cf16 rows\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 ,\cf16  cols\cf5 :\cf16  Int\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  Self\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # Seed the random number generator using the current time.\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         random\cf5 .\cf16 seed\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf5 var\cf16  data\cf5 :\cf16  List\cf5 [\cf16 List\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ]]\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 []\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf5 for\cf16  _ \cf5 in\cf16  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf16 rows\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf5 var\cf16  row_data\cf5 :\cf16  List\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 []\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf5 for\cf16  _ \cf5 in\cf16  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf16 cols\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 \cf6 # Generate a random 0 or 1 and append it to the row.\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 row_data\cf5 .\cf16 append\cf5 (\cf16 Int\cf5 (\cf16 random\cf5 .\cf16 random_si64\cf5 (\cf17 0\cf5 ,\cf16  \cf17 1\cf5 )))\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             data\cf5 .\cf16 append\cf5 (\cf16 row_data\cf5 ^)\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf16  Self\cf5 (\cf16 rows\cf5 ,\cf16  cols\cf5 ,\cf16  data\cf5 ^)\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 At the top of the file, we're importing the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 random
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  package from the Mojo standard library. It includes several functions related to random number generation.\
By default, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pseudorandom_number_generator"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 pseudorandom number generator}} used by the Mojo standard library currently uses a fixed seed. This means it generates the same sequence of numbers unless you provide a different seed, which is useful for testing purposes. However, for this application, we want to call 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 random.seed()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to set a seed value based on the current time, which gives us a unique value every time.\
Then we create 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as an empty 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[List[Int]]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which we'll populate with a random initial state. For each cell, we call {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/random/random/random_si64/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 random.random_si64()}}, which returns a random integer value from the provided minimum and maximum values of 0 and 1, respectively. This function actually returns a value of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which is a signed 64-bit integer value. As described in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/types/#numeric-types"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Numeric types}}, this is not the same as the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type, whose precision is dependent on the native word size of the system. Therefore, we're passing this value to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/Int/#__init__"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Int()}} constructor, which explicitly converts a numeric value to an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
After creating a complete row of random values, we append it to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  owns all its elements, so when we call 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 append()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , we need to decide whether to transfer ownership of the new row or provide a copy of it. In this case, we don't need to use the row again, so we use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  transfer sigil to transfer ownership of the row to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . (We didn't need to use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  sigil when appending the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values because they're implicitly copyable.)\
The return type of the method is 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which is an alias for the type of the struct. This is a convenient shortcut if the actual name of the struct is long or includes parameters. The last line uses 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Self()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to invoke the struct's constructor and return a newly created instance with random data. Once again, we use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  transfer sigil to transfer ownership of the newly created 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[List[Int]]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to the new instance rather than make a copy of it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 for
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  loops in the code above assign the loop value to "
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 _
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 ", the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/#explicit-lifetime-extension"}}{\fldrslt \ul discard pattern}}, to indicate that it's intentionally not used. Without this, the Mojo compiler would report a warning that the loop variable is unused.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

