\fs64 \cf2 12. Implement a method to evolve the grid\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 It's finally time to let our world evolve. We'll implement an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 evolve()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method to calculate the state of the grid for the next generation. One option would be to do an in-place modification of the existing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance. Instead, we'll have 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 evolve()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return a new instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for the next generation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf16 \cb22 gridv1.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf16  \cf14 Grid\cf5 (\cf16 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf16  Stringable\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15     \cf5 ...\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf16  \cf14 evolve\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 ->\cf16  Self\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15         next_generation \cf5 =\cf16  List\cf5 [\cf16 List\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ]]()\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf5 for\cf16  row \cf5 in\cf16  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 .\cf16 rows\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             row_data \cf5 =\cf16  List\cf5 [\cf16 Int\cf5 ]()\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15             \cf6 # Calculate neighboring row indices, handling "wrap-around"\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15             row_above \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 (\cf16 row \cf5 -\cf16  \cf17 1\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 %\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 rows\cb1 \
\cb15             row_below \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 (\cf16 row \cf5 +\cf16  \cf17 1\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 %\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 rows\cb1 \
\
\cb15             \cf5 for\cf16  col \cf5 in\cf16  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 .\cf16 cols\cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 \cf6 # Calculate neighboring column indices, handling "wrap-around"\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 col_left \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 (\cf16 col \cf5 -\cf16  \cf17 1\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 %\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 cols\cb1 \
\cb15                 col_right \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 (\cf16 col \cf5 +\cf16  \cf17 1\cf5 )\cf16  \cf5 %\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 cols\cb1 \
\
\cb15                 \cf6 # Determine number of populated cells around the current cell\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 num_neighbors \cf5 =\cf16  \cf5 (\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     self\cf5 [\cf16 row_above\cf5 ,\cf16  col_left\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     \cf5 +\cf16  self\cf5 [\cf16 row_above\cf5 ,\cf16  col\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     \cf5 +\cf16  self\cf5 [\cf16 row_above\cf5 ,\cf16  col_right\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     \cf5 +\cf16  self\cf5 [\cf16 row\cf5 ,\cf16  col_left\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     \cf5 +\cf16  self\cf5 [\cf16 row\cf5 ,\cf16  col_right\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     \cf5 +\cf16  self\cf5 [\cf16 row_below\cf5 ,\cf16  col_left\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     \cf5 +\cf16  self\cf5 [\cf16 row_below\cf5 ,\cf16  col\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     \cf5 +\cf16  self\cf5 [\cf16 row_below\cf5 ,\cf16  col_right\cf5 ]\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 \cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15                 \cf6 # Determine the state of the current cell for the next generation\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 new_state \cf5 =\cf16  \cf17 0\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 \cf5 if\cf16  self\cf5 [\cf16 row\cf5 ,\cf16  col\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf17 1\cf16  \cf5 and\cf16  \cf5 (\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     num_neighbors \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf17 2\cf16  \cf5 or\cf16  num_neighbors \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf17 3\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 \cf5 ):\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     new_state \cf5 =\cf16  \cf17 1\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 \cf5 elif\cf16  self\cf5 [\cf16 row\cf5 ,\cf16  col\cf5 ]\cf16  \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf17 0\cf16  \cf5 and\cf16  num_neighbors \cf5 ==\cf16  \cf17 3\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                     new_state \cf5 =\cf16  \cf17 1\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15                 row_data\cf5 .\cf16 append\cf5 (\cf16 new_state\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15             next_generation\cf5 .\cf16 append\cf5 (\cf16 row_data\cf5 ^)\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf16  Self\cf5 (\cf16 self\cf5 .\cf16 rows\cf5 ,\cf16  self\cf5 .\cf16 cols\cf5 ,\cf16  next_generation\cf5 ^)\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 We start with an empty 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[List[Int]]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to represent the state of the next generation. Then we use nested 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loops to iterate over each row and column of the existing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to determine the state of each cell in the next generation.\
For each cell in the grid, we need to count the number of populated neighboring cells. Because we're modeling the world as a toroid, we need to consider the top and bottom rows as adjacent and the leftmost and rightmost columns as adjacent. As we iterate through each row and column, we're using the modulo operator (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 %
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to handle "wrap-around" when we calculate the indices of the rows above and below and the columns to the left and right of the current cell. (For example, if there are 8 rows, then 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -1 % 8
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is 7.)\
Then we apply the Game of Life rules that determine whether the current cell is populated (1) or unpopulated (0) for the next generation:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
