\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 When you use Python code and packages as part of your Mojo program, you create a run-time dependency on a compatible Python runtime and packages. Building an executable version of your program with 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 mojo build
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  does not incorporate a Python runtime or Python packages into the resulting executable file. These run-time Python dependencies must be provided by the environment where you run the executable. The easiest way to ensure this requirement is met is to deploy and run your Mojo executable in a virtual environment, such as one managed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Pixi}} or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.conda.io/"}}{\fldrslt \ul conda}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can import a Python module in Mojo using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#import_module"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Python.import_module()}}. This returns a reference to the module in the form of a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  wrapper. You must store the reference in a variable so that you can then access the functions and objects in the module. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf16  std\cf5 .\cf16 python \cf5 import\cf16  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf16  \cf14 run_display\cf5 ()\cf16  raises\cf5 :\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb15     \cf6 # This is roughly equivalent to Python's `import pygame`\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     pygame \cf5 =\cf16  Python\cf5 .\cf16 import_module\cf5 (\cf23 "pygame"\cf5 )\cf16 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf6 # Initialize pygame modules\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15     pygame\cf5 .\cf16 init\cf5 ()\cf16 \cb1 \
