\cf2 Each argument for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 run_display()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  other than 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 grid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has a default value associated with it (for example, the default 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 window_height
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is 600 pixels). If you don't explicitly pass a value for an argument when you invoke 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 run_display()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , Mojo uses the default value specified in the function definition.\
After importing the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pygame
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  module, we call 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pygame.init()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to initialize all pygame subsystems.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 set_mode()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function creates and initializes a window with the width and height passed as a Python tuple of two values. This returns a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python_object/PythonObject/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 PythonObject}} wrapper for the window, which we can then use to call functions and set attributes to manipulate the window. (For more information about interacting with Python objects from Mojo, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/python/types/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Python types}}.)\
The bulk of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 run_display()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function is a loop that uses 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pygame
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to poll for events like key presses and mouse clicks. If it detects that the user presses 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 q
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 <Escape>
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  key or closes the display window, it ends the program with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pygame.quit()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Otherwise, it clears the window and iterates through all cells in the grid to display the populated cells. After sleeping for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pause
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  seconds, it evolves the grid to the next generation and loops again.\
